<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    BrowserSmokeFixture — the page the Phase 761 browser smoke harness drives.

    A Fable app, and deliberately not a sample: nothing here ships, and
    its only consumer is `ToolUp.BrowserSmoke.Tests`, which serves the
    bundled output over `FixtureHost` and drives it with Playwright.

    WHAT IS UNDER TEST is the shipped source, not a copy of it. The two
    `samples/MinimalClient` files compiled below are the Phase 535 / 760
    REFERENCE WIRING — the code a consumer is told to copy — so if that
    wiring rots the smoke gate goes red. `Fixture.fs` adds only the two
    things a deployment owns and the SDK deliberately does not: the
    module-owned HTTP API over `ICrdtDocumentStore` (Phase 535 is
    seam-first and mounts no route), and the fan-out that tells a tab a
    co-editor moved — polled here, where a deployment would subscribe to
    the reserved `_platform.crdt` notification topic.

    WHY IT ProjectReferences `ToolUp.Offline.Client` WHOLE, exactly as
    `samples/MinimalClient` does. The client tiers under test are not
    separable by hand: `SyncCoordinator` takes an `OfflineConfig`, which
    lives in `SDK.ClientTypes.fs`, which sits behind the folded Elmish
    tier — so a "compile just the four files" fixture reconstructs most
    of `ToolUp.Platform.Client` one `<Compile>` line at a time and then
    has to keep that order in step with the tier's own. Reusing the
    reference shape costs an npm install this gate pays for once, in its
    own job, and cannot drift.

    NOT IN ToolUp.Forge.sln, on purpose, exactly as MinimalClient is
    not: `dotnet build ToolUp.Forge.sln` and `VerifyAll` must be
    untouched by this phase. The harness has its own opt-in target,
    the `VerifyBrowserSmoke` target in Build.fs.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);output\**;node_modules\**;dist\**</DefaultItemExcludes>
    <!-- The Fable ecosystem (Feliz, the in-tree ToolUp.Elmish runtime,
         Fable.SimpleJson) pre-dates F# 10's nullable reference types;
         enabling nullness here would cascade "null / not null
         inconsistent" errors through the transitive references. Per the
         repo `CLAUDE.md`: nullness stays disabled on Fable-touching
         projects. -->
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The Phase 535 / 760 reference wiring, compiled from the samples
         themselves rather than restated here. Link-compiled rather than
         moved: `samples/MinimalClient` is the Fable ACCEPTANCE gate's
         consumer and stays that. -->
    <Compile Include="..\..\..\samples\MinimalClient\CrdtCoEditSample.fs" Link="Sample\CrdtCoEditSample.fs" />
    <Compile Include="..\..\..\samples\MinimalClient\CrdtOfflineCoEditSample.fs" Link="Sample\CrdtOfflineCoEditSample.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Fixture.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Phase 535's client pump arrives with this tier; Phase 24's
         IndexedDB queue and Phase 760's routed coordinator with the
         one below. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\ToolUp.Platform.Client\ToolUp.Platform.Client.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\src\ToolUp.Offline.Client\ToolUp.Offline.Client.fsproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
    <PackageReference Include="Fable.Core" />
    <PackageReference Include="Feliz" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
